1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,625
Hi! We're Team Zero Drift, from VIT Vellore, and

2
00:00:03,625 --> 00:00:06,999
this is Path Finder, for Samsung PRISM Track Three.

3
00:00:07,349 --> 00:00:11,401
Jammed sensor in. Full 6-DoF trajectory

4
00:00:11,401 --> 00:00:14,726
out. Even after the sensor dies.

5
00:00:15,876 --> 00:00:20,073
Picture a drone where GPS is jammed. All it has left is one IMU.

6
00:00:20,423 --> 00:00:23,429
And the jamming gets worse every minute. Noise

7
00:00:23,429 --> 00:00:26,762
climbs, samples freeze, rows go missing. And in the

8
00:00:26,762 --> 00:00:29,963
last five seconds, the sensor is completely dead.

9
00:00:30,313 --> 00:00:32,203
Integrate that the regular way, and

10
00:00:32,203 --> 00:00:33,985
you land about 2 kilometres away.

11
00:00:35,135 --> 00:00:37,454
Let's run it, live. We degrade a real

12
00:00:37,454 --> 00:00:40,086
recording with the organisers' own script.

13
00:00:40,436 --> 00:00:43,113
Then, just one command. Pure Python,

14
00:00:43,113 --> 00:00:46,236
standard library only. Nothing to install.

15
00:00:46,586 --> 00:00:49,425
A hundred and forty two seconds of

16
00:00:49,425 --> 00:00:52,013
data, done in just 0.2 seconds.

17
00:00:52,363 --> 00:00:55,335
Every input row gets a pose, in exact

18
00:00:55,335 --> 00:00:58,387
TUM format, and the validator says OK.

19
00:00:58,737 --> 00:01:03,351
Budget check: under one megabyte of heap, against a limit of

20
00:01:03,351 --> 00:01:07,889
eight. 1.4 milliseconds per second of data, against twenty.

21
00:01:09,039 --> 00:01:13,521
So how does it work? First, it measures the file itself: column

22
00:01:13,521 --> 00:01:17,647
order, units, sample rate and gravity. Nothing is assumed.

23
00:01:17,997 --> 00:01:20,968
Then it repairs dead, stuck and spiky

24
00:01:20,968 --> 00:01:24,179
rows, and tracks attitude from the gyro.

25
00:01:24,529 --> 00:01:27,501
And here's the trick. On a multirotor, rotor drag

26
00:01:27,501 --> 00:01:30,715
makes the sideways accelerometer reading proportional

27
00:01:30,715 --> 00:01:33,444
to speed. So physics becomes our speedometer.

28
00:01:33,794 --> 00:01:36,284
A backward smoother lets the future correct

29
00:01:36,284 --> 00:01:38,832
the past. And when the sensor dies, the path

30
00:01:38,832 --> 00:01:41,264
relaxes toward where the vehicle has been.

31
00:01:42,414 --> 00:01:45,968
Here it is on a handheld recording. Left, the raw

32
00:01:45,968 --> 00:01:50,031
signal. Right, what we recover, drawn over ground truth.

33
00:01:50,381 --> 00:01:53,530
Now the IMU dies, and we keep tracking,

34
00:01:53,530 --> 00:01:55,871
blind, for five full seconds.

35
00:01:57,021 --> 00:02:00,656
On four held-out sequences we never touched while building, we

36
00:02:00,656 --> 00:02:04,114
beat a tracker that never moves in eight runs out of eight.

37
00:02:04,464 --> 00:02:08,191
Fresh random seeds, both column orders: twenty out of twenty,

38
00:02:08,191 --> 00:02:11,552
and up to forty five percent better on handheld motion.

39
00:02:11,902 --> 00:02:14,654
Seven times under the memory limit. Thirteen

40
00:02:14,654 --> 00:02:17,094
times under compute. Zero dependencies.

41
00:02:18,244 --> 00:02:22,060
Where does it matter? GPS denied drones, exactly the brief.

42
00:02:22,410 --> 00:02:28,185
XR headsets, like Galaxy XR, when the cameras lose tracking

43
00:02:28,185 --> 00:02:33,764
in the dark. We keep the pose alive until vision returns.

44
00:02:34,114 --> 00:02:39,613
And tiny wearables, with no room for numpy, GPUs or big models.

45
00:02:40,763 --> 00:02:43,966
We stay honest. Every number is compared with doing

46
00:02:43,966 --> 00:02:47,546
nothing, and every decision is logged, even the failures.

47
00:02:47,896 --> 00:02:49,717
Team Zero Drift. Thank you!

